# OzzBooks — Addendum: Comics & graphic novels support

This addendum extends `Claude.md` with a planned (not yet built) third
content pillar alongside audiobooks and ebooks: native support for comic
issues and graphic novels. OzzBooks itself only ever ingests `.cbz`
(plus, as fast-follows, loose image folders and a minority of PDFs) —
`.cbr` is handled entirely outside this codebase; see the corrected
decision below and `Ozzbooks_Addendum_CbrToCbzConversion`. Keep this
alongside `Claude.md` and the other addenda so future sessions have
this context.

**Status: planned, not started.** Discussed and scoped 2026-09-02.
Supersedes the shorter "Speculative, not committed — comics/graphic
novel support" bullet in `Claude.md`'s Future phases section — that
bullet should be replaced with a pointer to this file once this is
committed to.

**Decisions locked in during scoping (2026-09-02):**
1. **Scope: the whole Comics library, not just the stray files.**
   `Claude.md` already flagged a real second library sitting unconnected
   to OzzBooks: `/Volumes/Books/Comics` on the same NAS, 67 series
   folders, ~3,440 files (mostly `.cbr`/`.cbz`, some loose `.png`/`.jpg`
   sequences, some `.pdf` graphic novels). This is being scoped as a
   real third pillar — new source, real series-grouping UI, sized for
   the full library — not a small fix for the handful of misfiled
   Dresden Files/Expanse Origins files that originally surfaced this.
2. **`.cbz` is the only format OzzBooks itself ingests — `.cbr` is out
   of scope for this codebase entirely.** Corrected 2026-09-02,
   superseding the original plan on this page (an in-app `unrar`
   shell-out at ingestion time, mirroring `mobiConvert.ts`'s `.mobi` →
   `.epub` conversion). Reasoning for the reversal: unlike mobi→epub,
   converting `.cbr` also required a structural special-case threaded
   through `scan.ts`'s matching logic (stored `file_path` can never
   equal the original `.cbr` path, so lookups need to go by
   content-hash instead of path, same shape as the existing `isMobi`
   branch) — real, working risk to the shared scan loop for a problem
   that has a much simpler answer: **convert `.cbr` → `.cbz` externally,
   as a NAS file-maintenance task**, the same category of work as the
   audiobook merging/renaming pass already happening in a separate
   session, not something OzzBooks' own ingestion pipeline needs to
   solve. See "CBR handling" below for exactly what this means for
   ingestion, and `Ozzbooks_Addendum_CbrToCbzConversion` for the
   separate conversion effort itself.

## Why comics are a different shape than the existing two formats

- **No text reflow.** Audio has timestamps, ebooks have CFIs from
  reflowable text. A comic page is a fixed-layout image — "position" is
  just page N of M, and the interesting UI problem is *fitting* an image
  to the screen, not flowing text into it.
- **Real estate matters more than it does today.** iPad landscape has
  room for a two-page spread; phone portrait wants single-page
  fit-height with pinch-zoom for panel detail. Pinch/pan is a genuinely
  new interaction — neither the audio player nor `EbookReader.tsx` needs
  it.
- **Metadata quality varies, and the real library is graphic-novel-
  heavy, not numbered single-issue runs.** `.cbr`/`.cbz` files can carry
  a `ComicInfo.xml` (the ComicRack/ComicTagger standard) with series,
  issue number, writer, penciller, publisher, year — a real asset when
  present. But Jim's actual library (confirmed 2026-09-02) is mostly
  **collected graphic novels organized by franchise folder** ("all
  Batman graphic novels together"), not sequential numbered issues like
  Dresden Files — closer in shape to a loosely-ordered book series than
  to comic-book-continuity numbering. Expect `ComicInfo.xml` presence/
  quality to be inconsistent across a scanned collection like this, the
  same real-world pattern already documented for audiobook genre tags
  ("usable on well under 20%" per `Claude.md`'s Phase 2b notes) — so
  **folder structure, which Jim has already deliberately curated, is
  the primary series signal**, not the embedded tag. See Ingestion
  changes below for exactly how that's derived.
- **`.cbr` is RAR, not zip, and OzzBooks never needs to care.** Node has
  no reliable pure-JS RAR reader — rather than take on an `unrar`
  shell-out inside the app (see the corrected decision above), `.cbr`
  handling is pushed entirely outside this codebase. `.cbz` is just a
  zip — trivially readable with `jszip`, already a **devDependency** in
  `server/package.json` (currently only used to build test fixtures)
  and needing promotion to a real dependency, but now purely a *read*
  dependency (parsing an existing `.cbz` to serve pages) rather than
  also a *write* dependency (building one from RAR-extracted files) —
  a smaller, lower-risk footprint than originally planned.

## Scope

**Adds:**
- A new source pointed at `/Volumes/Books/Comics` — reuses the existing
  `sources.type` values (`'synology'`/`'local'`, whichever matches how
  it's actually mounted), same as the two "Ozzstation" audio/ebook
  sources today; no new source `type` needed, just a different
  `path_scope`.
- `.cbz` as a new `books.format` value (CHECK constraint update). This
  is the **only** archive format OzzBooks itself ingests — see "CBR
  handling" below.
- A `page_count` column on `books`.
- A third `position.type` value, `'page'` (value = page index), joining
  the existing `'timestamp' | 'cfi'` union already used by
  `progress`/`bookmarks`.
- A new ingestion module, new page-serving route, and a new
  `ComicReader.tsx` frontend page — see below.

**Explicitly deferred to a fast-follow, not core v1:**
- **PDF graphic novels** (a real minority in the actual NAS library).
  The reader UI could plausibly be shared (render each PDF page to an
  image via `pdf.js` and feed it through the same paged-image viewer),
  but the extraction path is different enough (no re-zip step, pages
  rendered on demand rather than read from a zip entry) that it
  shouldn't block shipping the core `.cbr`/`.cbz` path. Revisit once the
  archive-based path is working end to end.
- **Loose image-sequence folders** (a comic scanned as a folder of
  `page001.jpg`, `page002.jpg`, ... with no archive at all). This is the
  comics equivalent of `mp3_folder` (audio without an M4B container) —
  same shape of problem, likely a `comic_folder` format value inferring
  page order from filename/track order. Worth doing, but only after
  confirming from real NAS data how common this actually is versus a
  handful of one-off exports.
- **Panel-guided reading** ("Guided View" in ComiXology's terms) —
  auto-detect individual panels on a page and let the reader tap/arrow
  through them one at a time, zoomed in, instead of viewing a whole page
  at once. Discussed 2026-09-02; see its own section below.
- **Swipe/drag page-turning** as an alternative to v1's tap zones —
  discussed 2026-09-02, see the reading-screen section below.

**Does not change:** the existing audio/ebook ingestion, streaming, or
reader code paths. This is additive — a third parallel pipeline, not a
generalization of the existing two.

## Data model changes

```
books
  format TEXT ... CHECK (format IN ('m4b', 'mp3_folder', 'epub', 'cbz'))
  page_count INTEGER  -- comics only; null for audio/epub

progress / bookmarks
  position { type: 'timestamp' | 'cfi' | 'page', value }
```

No new `pages` table — a zip's central directory already gives page
order and count for free; pages are addressed by index at serve time,
not pre-extracted into rows. `series_name`/`series_number` (and
`series_number_source`) are reused as-is — a graphic novel/issue is a
series entry, same columns, same manual-edit UI already on Book Detail
for audiobooks. See Ingestion changes below for how those get derived
for comics specifically (folder-first, not tag-first — corrected
2026-09-02 once the real library's shape was clarified).

## Ingestion changes

New `server/src/ingestion/comic.ts`:
- Parse `ComicInfo.xml` from the archive root when present: series,
  issue number, title, writer, penciller, publisher, year, page count,
  summary.
- **Series name: folder-derived by default, tag overrides only when
  present and legitimate** — the reverse precedence from what was
  originally assumed here, corrected 2026-09-02 once the real library's
  shape was clarified (see "Why comics are a different shape" above).
  Concretely: **series name = the first path segment of the file's path
  relative to the source's `path_scope`** — not the file's immediate
  parent folder. This matters because the real structure can nest
  deeper than one level (confirmed 2026-09-02): `Comics/Batman/some-
  gn.cbr` and `Comics/Batman/Elseworlds/some-gn.cbr` must both resolve
  to series "Batman," not fragment into "Batman" and "Elseworlds" as
  two separate series. A file sitting directly at the source root with
  no subfolder at all gets no folder-derived series — same "Not part of
  a series" bucket `groupBySeries` already has for audiobooks. This
  lines up with `Claude.md`'s original scoping note that the real NAS
  library has "67 series folders" — that count only makes sense if
  depth-1 folders are themselves the series unit.
- **Series number: `series_number_source` (`'tag' | 'folder' |
  'manual'`), already in the schema for exactly this precedence,
  reused as-is.** `'tag'` = `ComicInfo.xml`'s `Number`/`Volume` field;
  `'folder'` = a leading "Vol 02 -" style prefix parsed the same way
  `seriesNumber.ts` already parses audiobook filenames; `'manual'` = the
  existing inline-edit UI on Book Detail, which — per that column's
  existing contract — is never overwritten by a later automatic guess.
  **Expect this to commonly be null for graphic novels** — a franchise
  folder full of trades has no inherent "issue number" the way a
  numbered single-issue run does. That's fine, not an error case:
  ordering falls back to title within the series, exactly like
  `compareBySeriesThenTitle` already does for audiobook series today.
  A publication-year tiebreaker (from `ComicInfo.xml`'s `Year`, before
  falling back to title) is worth adding as a small parameterization of
  that same shared sort function, since it'd get "closer to right"
  automatically for an unnumbered franchise — not required for v1.
- Cover art: first page image (natural-sort the zip's file list, not
  alphabetic — `page2.jpg` must sort before `page10.jpg`), piped through
  the existing artwork resize pipeline (`sharp` is already a dependency).

**v1 blocker, found 2026-09-02 auditing the real code: `enrichBooks.ts`
needs an explicit format exclusion.** Its candidate query is `WHERE
status = 'active' AND metadata_enrichment_attempted_at IS NULL` — no
format filter at all today. Left as-is, every newly-ingested comic
becomes a candidate for the Open Library genre/synopsis/cover backfill
pass, which is a prose-book search API — "Batman: Hush" or "The Long
Halloween" could easily match an unrelated novel and silently write a
wrong genre/synopsis/cover onto a comic, which is worse than leaving it
unenriched. Add `AND format != 'cbz'` (or equivalent) to that query
before comics ship, not as a fast-follow.

## CBR handling

Corrected 2026-09-02 — see the reversed decision at the top of this
document. OzzBooks' own ingestion never shells out to `unrar` or
converts anything; `.cbr` handling is:

- **`findCandidates` still recognizes `.cbr` files, but only to skip and
  log them, never to ingest them.** A `.cbr` found during a scan is not
  silently invisible — it's routed through `scan_issues` with a clear,
  actionable message ("still `.cbr` — convert to `.cbz` to add this to
  the library"), the same visibility mechanism the app already gives
  any other file it can't use. Given the real NAS library is
  meaningfully `.cbr`-heavy today, this list effectively becomes a free
  progress tracker for the separate conversion effort (see
  `Ozzbooks_Addendum_CbrToCbzConversion`) — no separate tool needed to
  answer "how much is left to convert."
- **Detect by magic bytes, not just extension** (`PK\x03\x04` for zip,
  `Rar!\x1a\x07` for RAR) — cheap, and rescues the rare mislabeled file
  (a `.cbr`-extensioned file that's actually already zip data, or vice
  versa) from sitting uselessly in the "needs conversion" bucket when
  it's already readable as-is.
- **No sequencing dependency between ingestion and conversion.**
  OzzBooks scans and shows whatever's already `.cbz` immediately;
  nightly rescans pick up newly-converted files automatically as the
  separate conversion pass progresses, the same "rescan finds a new
  candidate" mechanism everything else already uses. The library fills
  in progressively rather than needing conversion finished first — this
  doesn't reverse the earlier "whole library, not just stray files"
  scope decision, it just changes how full coverage is reached.

## Serving changes

- A new route, e.g. `GET /api/books/:id/pages/:index`, opens the book's
  `.cbz` and streams that single page's image bytes — same idea as
  chapter-file streaming today, but zip-entry-addressed by index instead
  of filesystem-path-addressed. No conversion-cache path to consider —
  every ingested comic's `file_path` is a real `.cbz` (see CBR handling
  above).
- Needs an in-memory or on-disk cache of "already-opened archive →
  sorted entry list" per book to avoid re-reading and re-sorting the zip
  central directory on every single page request during a reading
  session — a real perf concern given some issues run 30+ pages and a
  reader flips through them in seconds.

## Frontend changes: the reading screen

Discussed in more detail 2026-09-02. New `app/src/pages/ComicReader.tsx`,
peer to `EbookReader.tsx` but deliberately different in a few ways the
ebook reader doesn't need to care about:

- **Immersive canvas, not a chrome-first screen.** `EbookReader.tsx`
  keeps a thin top bar always visible, which is fine there since it
  never competes with a full page of art. A comic page *is* the content
  edge-to-edge, so the top bar + page counter here **auto-hide** after a
  couple seconds and get summoned back with a tap, canvas going fully
  edge-to-edge in between. Background behind the page (and any
  letterboxing when a page's aspect ratio doesn't fill the screen) is
  **black**, not the app's light/dark theme colors — deliberately reads
  as a dedicated photo/comic viewer (matches Comixology, Apple Books'
  image mode, most manga readers), not an extension of the app chrome.
- **Three-way tap zones**, not the ebook reader's two edge-strips: left
  third / right third for prev/next, **center third summons the
  chrome** (since chrome isn't passively visible here the way the ebook
  reader's is, it needs a reliable, accidental-proof way back).
- **v1 page-turn gesture: tap zones only** — same proven, low-risk
  pattern as the ebook reader, no new gesture code needed to ship.
  **Fast-follow: add swipe/drag as an alternative** once the tap-based
  reader is live and being used for real — swipe needs its own gesture
  handling (nothing to reuse from the ebook reader) and has to be
  disambiguated from the pinch-zoom pan gesture below, so it's worth
  doing as a deliberate second pass rather than bundling into the first
  ship.
- **Fit-to-screen (contain) by default, not fit-width.** Fit-width often
  means scrolling vertically within a single page to see the bottom
  half, which fights the one-tap-equals-one-page model. Fit-to-screen
  keeps the whole page visible at once; pinch-to-zoom + double-tap-to-
  zoom (standard convention: double-tap zooms ~2x centered on the tap
  point, tap again to reset) is how you get to panel-level detail. While
  zoomed, swipe pans instead of turning the page — needs an explicit
  "currently zoomed" state (e.g. a small reset-zoom control appears in
  the chrome) so it's never ambiguous what a swipe does next. This is
  all new gesture handling — nothing to reuse from the ebook reader.
- **Jump-to-page thumbnail strip.** Ebooks don't benefit from this (a
  CFI list means nothing visually), but comic pages are instantly
  recognizable at a glance — a horizontal filmstrip of page thumbnails
  in the chrome, tap one to jump straight there, is a much better
  "find that splash page again" tool than a page-number slider.
- **Spread mode is opportunistic, not default** — only offered when the
  viewport is wide enough that two portrait pages side by side wouldn't
  be tiny (realistically iPad landscape only); phone stays single-page
  at any orientation.
- Reading-direction toggle (left-to-right vs right-to-left) — the real
  library here is Western comics (Dresden Files, Expanse) so
  left-to-right is the correct default, but the toggle is cheap to build
  now and avoids a bigger retrofit if any right-to-left material shows
  up later.
- Position tracking: current page index, synced through the existing
  `progress` API extended for `type: 'page'` — same debounced-save
  pattern `EbookReader.tsx` already uses for CFIs.
- Offline: whole-issue download only, not per-page granularity — see
  its own section below (the storage-budget story turned out to need
  more than "reuse what's there as-is").
- **v1 gap, found 2026-09-02: no page-load-failure/retry design.**
  Everything above covers fit/zoom/chrome/tap-zones, but never designed
  what happens when a single page request fails mid-read (a network
  blip, the Mac mini hiccuping). Audio has an explicit self-healing
  policy for exactly this — check IndexedDB first, retry the stream a
  few times with backoff, only surface an error if all of that fails
  (per `Claude.md`'s stream-failure fallback). The comic reader needs
  the equivalent: on a failed page fetch, check the `comicPages` cache
  first (the opportunistic pre-fetch means the next page or two is
  often already there), then retry the network request with backoff,
  and only then show an in-chrome "couldn't load this page, retry"
  state — never leave a blank page with no explanation and no way
  forward.
- Library UI: see its own section below.
- Book Detail page: see its own section below.
- Offline download experience: see its own section below.

## Book Detail page for a comic

Discussed 2026-09-02, against the current `BookDetail.tsx`. Most of the
page reuses existing pieces unchanged; a few things swap for
comics-appropriate fields, and one thing is new.

**Reused as-is:**
- Cover art, the missing-book banner + relink flow, "Add to My Library"
  toggle, "Remove from In Progress," source label, and the synopsis
  block (`ComicInfo.xml`'s `Summary` field maps straight onto the
  existing `book.synopsis` display).
- **The series name/number inline-edit UI, completely unchanged.** The
  existing `editingSeries`/`seriesNameDraft`/`seriesNumberDraft` block
  already edits exactly `series_name`/`series_number` — precisely what
  a comic issue needs (series + issue #). No new component.
- **`AddToPlaylist`, enabled** — see `Ozzbooks_Addendum_PlaylistsForReading`
  for the full fix (this was wrongly assumed hidden-by-design earlier
  in this same discussion; it turns out to be a pre-existing ebook gap,
  not a deliberate audio-only restriction, and comics should ship with
  it working rather than inheriting the same gap).

**Changed:**
- **Narrator inline-edit → Writer/Artist.** The narrator block
  (`book.format !== 'epub' && ...`) is audio-specific; comics show
  `Writer: X · Artist: Y` from `ComicInfo.xml` (the same two credits
  already chosen as Publisher/Writer facets in the Library & browsing UI
  plan). Genre stays, reusing `GENRE_OPTIONS` as-is for now — not worth
  inventing a comics-specific vocabulary before seeing whether the
  existing prose-tuned list actually feels wrong in practice.
- **Duration → page count.** The `{book.totalDuration > 0 && ...}` row
  becomes "32 pages" — same slot, same zero-guard pattern, different
  field.
- **Primary action button simplifies** — comics never have a companion
  (no audio+ebook dual-button case to handle), so it's just `Keep
  Reading`/`Start Reading` → `/book/:id/read`, the same route pattern
  the epub reader already hangs off `book.format === 'epub'` for, just
  mounting `ComicReader` instead based on `format === 'cbz'`.
- **Download badge becomes single-toggle, not per-chapter.** Whole-issue
  downloads only (see Frontend changes above) makes this closer to
  `EbookDownloadBadge`'s "cached or not, no partial state" shape than
  `DownloadBadge`'s per-chapter list — even though under the hood it's
  fetching N page images, the user only ever sees one aggregate
  cached/not-cached/downloading state.
- **The chapters list at the bottom is simply absent.** Comics have no
  `chapters` rows at all (pages are addressed by index straight from
  the archive per the Data model section above) — nothing to render
  there.

**New:** a **"More in this series"** strip — nearby issue covers
(previous/current/next, or the whole run if short), each tappable
straight to *that* issue's own Book Detail page. Deliberately mirrors
existing behavior rather than inventing a shortcut: everywhere else in
the app (`BookTile`/`BookRow`), tapping a cover goes to Detail, not
straight into playback/reading, so this strip does the same rather than
jumping directly into the reader. This is the one thing audiobooks have
no real equivalent of — "next issue" is a one-tap browsing gesture for
comics in a way "next audiobook in a series" isn't.

## Library & browsing UI

Discussed 2026-09-02. `Library.tsx` today handles My Library and Store
as two routes sharing one component, with view modes (List / By Author
/ By Series), tile/row display, faceted filters (Status, Format,
Source, Genre, Narrator via `FilterSheet.tsx`), and an "In Progress"
shelf mixing audio+ebook progress.

**Correction to an earlier decision in this same discussion**: a
dedicated bottom-nav "Comics" tab was floated and initially preferred,
but reconsidered — the bottom nav already carries 5 tabs (Store,
Library, Playlists, Now Playing, Settings), and a 6th makes an already
narrow phone nav bar tighter for no real gain over the alternative
below. Settled instead on:

**A content-type toggle at the top of the existing Library/Store
screens**, not a new route or nav entry: a segmented control — "Books"
(today's audio+ebook grid, unchanged) vs. "Comics" — sitting just under
the page title, above the search/sort/filter row. My Library vs. Store
stays exactly as it is today (still the two routes, still about
ownership); the new toggle is an orthogonal axis, scoping *which*
collection either route is browsing. So the four real states are "My
Library → Books" (today's default), "My Library → Comics", "Store →
Books" (today's default), "Store → Comics" — no new routes needed,
`App.tsx`'s existing `/library`/`/store` → `<Library />` wiring is
unchanged.

**State**: a new `contentType: 'books' | 'comics'` value in
`LibraryViewContext`, same plain-React-state-above-the-router pattern
already used for `viewMode`/`displayMode`/the filter sets — persists
across navigating away and back, same reason those do today (Route
unmount/remount would otherwise reset it).

**What actually changes when the toggle flips:**
- **Default view mode switches with it**: Books keeps today's default
  (`List`); Comics defaults to `By Series` — given 67 franchise-organized
  series folders covering most of ~3,440 files, series-first is clearly
  the right default browsing shape here, unlike the mostly-standalone
  audio/ebook collection. (Ordering *within* a series often falls back
  to title rather than a real issue number, same as audiobooks today —
  see Ingestion changes above — but that doesn't change which grouping
  mode is the sane default, only how each group sorts internally.)
  Explicit switch, not just "whatever the last mode was" — a
  bySeries-tuned mode is close to useless for a 90%-standalone audio/
  ebook collection and vice versa. **See "Series-browsing view" below —
  comics' By Series can't just be the audiobook By Series view reused
  verbatim**, since inline-expanding all 67 series' full grids on one
  page doesn't hold up at this scale.
- **Facets swap, they don't just filter down.** `FilterSheet.tsx`'s
  Genre/Narrator facets don't mean anything for comics (no narrator;
  genre optional/lower-value there); Format is moot (everything in the
  Comics view is, well, a comic). Comics mode instead offers
  **Publisher** and **Writer/Artist** (from `ComicInfo.xml`, stored the
  same way audiobook `author`/`narrator` already are) as its facets.
  Status and Source stay in both modes — page-position-derived Status
  works identically to today's chapter/CFI-derived version, and Source
  still matters once/if a comic ever comes from more than one place.
  Net effect: `FilterSheet` takes a facet *set* keyed by `contentType`,
  not a fixed prop list — Genre/Narrator/Format's props simply aren't
  passed when `contentType === 'comics'`, Publisher/Writer's are.
- **The "In Progress" shelf filters by the active toggle too** — someone
  reopening Library to continue an audiobook shouldn't see a mid-issue
  comic thumbnail interleaved with it, and vice versa. Same underlying
  progress data, just partitioned by `contentType` before rendering,
  the same way it's already partitioned by `bookInLibrary`/removed-from-
  shelf today.
- **Tile treatment**: reuse `BookTile`/`BookRow`/`BookGrid` as-is rather
  than building parallel components — just swap the subtitle line
  (issue number + series instead of author) and the format badge (a
  comics-specific glyph parallel to `FormatBadge`'s 🎧/📖) when rendering
  a comic. `dedupeCompanionPairs` doesn't apply (comics have no
  audio/ebook companion concept), so that step is simply skipped for
  the Comics collection.
- **Search box, sort dropdown, tile/row toggle stay shared UI** — same
  component, same interaction, just operating over whichever collection
  the content-type toggle currently selects.
- **v1 fix, found 2026-09-02 auditing the real code: search needs to
  match `series_name`, not just title/author.** `Library.tsx`'s filter
  predicate today is `query && !title.includes(query) &&
  !author.includes(query)` — `series_name` was never part of it, for
  any format. That gap barely matters for audiobooks (the title usually
  carries enough on its own), but it matters a lot here: "Batman" is
  the primary mental model for this library, and a volume titled just
  "Hush" has zero chance of surfacing on a "Batman" search without this
  fix. Add `series_name` to the same predicate — a one-line change,
  benefits both content types, not comics-specific plumbing.

## Series-browsing view

Discussed 2026-09-02. Today's audiobook "By Series" view inline-expands
every series' full book grid, stacked down one page. With 67 series
averaging ~51 files each, that model breaks down completely for comics
— up to 3,440 tiles rendered in one unbroken scroll, both unusable to
browse and a real performance concern: `CoverArt.tsx`'s `<img>` has no
`loading="lazy"` and `BookGrid` does no virtualization at all today,
so an unfiltered flat view means requesting thousands of cover images
at once. That's already a latent cost at today's ~5,470-book library;
comics is what actually makes it bite. **Worth fixing regardless of
comics** — add `loading="lazy"` to `CoverArt`'s `<img>`, a one-line,
no-downside change that helps both this new view and the existing flat
Library.

So comics' By Series needs **two levels**, not the audiobook pattern
reused as-is:

**Level 1 — series cards.** The By Series landing view shows one card
per series: cover (the lowest-`series_number`/earliest-by-fallback-
order book's cover, by convention), series name, item count, and an
**aggregate progress indicator** ("12 of 45 read") — genuinely new
computation, not something `groupBySeries` needs today since it only
clusters tiles, never reasons about status across them. For Jim's
actual library this literally is "the Batman card," "the Expanse
card," etc.

**Level 2 — a new Series Detail page**, opened by tapping a card: a
grid of just that series' items, ordered using the same fallback chain
as the rest of this plan (`series_number` when one of tag/folder/manual
supplied it, else title — see Ingestion changes) rather than assuming a
strict numeric sequence exists. Tapping an item still goes to its own
Book Detail page, not straight into the reader — consistent with the
"More in this series" strip decision and with how `BookTile`/`BookRow`
already behave everywhere else in the app.

**Real wrinkle: series aren't a normalized table.** `series_name` is a
denormalized string column on `books`, so Series Detail's route has to
key off that string (URL-encoded), not a stable id. Functionally fine,
but it means editing a series' name (already possible via the existing
inline-edit UI) effectively changes that series' URL — never mattered
before because the old By Series view never linked anywhere; it does
now.

Standalone/one-shot graphic novels reuse the existing convention
exactly: a lone book under a "series" folds into a flat "Not part of a
series" bucket, same as `groupBySeries` already does today.

**Fast-follows, not required for v1:**
- A jump-to-item-number control for very long runs — worth doing once
  real data shows how long the longest series in this library actually
  are, not worth guessing at now.
- **Sub-headings within a series from deeper folder nesting.** Since
  series identity is pinned at the first path segment (see Ingestion
  changes), a real nested structure like `Batman/Elseworlds/` doesn't
  have to be thrown away — that second segment could become an optional
  sub-heading grouping those particular items together within the
  Batman Series Detail page, a display nicety layered on top of the
  same series identity rather than either ignoring the nesting or
  wrongly promoting it to its own top-level series. The raw path
  information is already available at ingestion time either way, so
  this is cheap to add later without a re-ingestion.

## Offline download experience

Discussed 2026-09-02, grounded in what the offline code actually does
today rather than what `Claude.md` summarizes it as doing.

**Finding: the storage-budget/LRU eviction model isn't actually
format-wide today.** `downloadManager.ts`'s `ensureBudget()` and
`getTotalCachedBytes()` only ever sum over the `audioFiles` IndexedDB
store. `useEbookDownload.ts` has **no budget check or eviction at
all** — it fetches and stores an epub unconditionally. This has been
harmless in practice because epub files are small; it would not be
harmless for comics, where a single graphic novel's page images can
plausibly run tens to over a hundred MB, closer to audiobook-chapter
sizes than epub-file sizes. Shipping comics downloads by copying the
epub pattern verbatim would mean someone downloading a handful of
issues before a trip could blow straight through their configured
`storage_budget_mb` with zero eviction, silently consuming real device
storage until iOS's own unpredictable eviction kicks in — exactly the
scenario the budget system exists to avoid.

**Decision: unify storage-budget accounting across all formats now,
rather than giving comics its own isolated check.** `ensureBudget()`/
`getTotalCachedBytes()` (`downloadManager.ts`) generalize to sum across
`audioFiles` + a new `comicPages` store + `epubFiles` (fixing the
existing epub gap as a side effect of the same change), evicting the
globally least-recently-used item regardless of format when a new
download needs room. This touches code the existing audio download
feature depends on, so it needs real care and its own test coverage
(per the Testing section below), not just a drive-by generalization.

**New `comicPages` IndexedDB store** (`db.ts`, schema version bump),
keyed by `(bookId, pageIndex)`, parallel to `audioFiles`/`epubFiles`.
Eviction granularity is **whole-issue, not per-page** — every cached
page for a `bookId` is pulled together, keyed by a book-level
`lastReadAt` timestamp, mirroring how `deleteCachedAudioFilesForBook`
already does bulk book-level deletion for audio's "remove all" action.
There's no such thing as evicting "half a comic" — matches the
whole-issue-download-only decision from the reading-screen plan above.

**Download flow**: "download whole issue" fetches all `page_count` page
endpoints and stores each blob, refreshing the UI's cached-count after
each page lands (not just once at the end) — same incremental-progress
pattern `useDownloads.downloadAll()` already uses per audio chapter —
feeding the single aggregate cached/downloading/not-cached badge state
already planned for Book Detail.

**Completion is tracked explicitly, not inferred by counting cached
blobs.** Audio's per-chapter model tolerates partial caching by design
— that's the point of chapter-level downloads. Comics deliberately
aren't supposed to expose a partial state to the user, but a download
that fails or gets interrupted mid-issue does leave a genuinely partial
set of cached pages behind. Without an explicit completion flag
(alongside the expected `page_count`), the UI can't distinguish "fully
downloaded" from "died at page 14 of 30" — store a boolean, don't infer
it from a blob count.

**Opportunistic pre-fetch while actively reading, independent of the
explicit download action.** Audio already background-caches the
current + next 1–2 chapters for resilience against short offline gaps,
per `Claude.md`'s stated design priority ("short offline gaps, not
extended offline"). Page images are typically small enough that doing
the same for comics — silently caching the next few pages while
someone's actively reading, with no explicit download tap required —
costs little and buys the same resilience. The reader's per-page fetch
resolution should be cache-first-unconditionally, the same pattern
`EbookReader.tsx`/`PlayerContext` already use for their own content:
check IndexedDB first, fall back to network, regardless of
connectivity state.

**Storage visibility breaks down by content type**, not just one
aggregate number — the existing `navigator.storage.estimate()` screen
(per `Claude.md`'s Phase 1 scope) should show audio/ebook/comics
separately once comics are a real presence, so "why did my audiobooks
get evicted" has an answerable "comics used the budget" rather than
being a mystery.

**Deliberately deferred, not core v1**: a "download whole series" bulk
action from the Series Detail page (handy before a trip — grab the next
few volumes at once). Real risk of someone one-tapping a 50-volume
series into instantly consuming the entire storage budget; doing that
safely needs its own size-estimate/confirmation UX, easier to design
once the single-issue download path is proven in real use.

## Fast-follow: panel-guided ("Guided View") reading

Discussed 2026-09-02, prompted by comic apps (ComiXology's "Guided
View," some manga readers' "Smart Panel" mode) that locate the first
panel on a page, zoom in on it, and let the reader tap/arrow through
each panel in reading order instead of viewing the whole page at once.
Deliberately scoped as a fast-follow **after** the core paged reader
(fit-height + pinch-zoom) ships and has been used for real against the
3,440-file library — not part of core v1.

**Two ways to get panel boundaries, very different reliability:**
1. *Embedded panel metadata* (e.g. ACBF-style per-panel bounding boxes,
   authored by whoever produced the file — this is how ComiXology's own
   Guided View actually works, hand-marked by their pipeline). Real
   library files here are scanned/scene-released `.cbr`s — essentially
   none of them will carry this. Not a realistic primary path.
2. *Automatic detection at ingestion time* — analyze each page image,
   find the gutters (whitespace/black bands separating panels) via a
   projection-profile algorithm, derive bounding boxes + reading order.
   [Kumiko](https://github.com/njean42/kumiko) is established open-source
   prior art here (Python/OpenCV), already used by manga-reader projects.
   Works well on clean, gutter-separated layouts; degrades on splash
   pages, bleeding art, or borderless panels — false positives/negatives
   are expected, not an edge case to design away.

**How it fits the existing architecture:**
- Detection runs **once at ingestion**, not on-device — same posture as
  Calibre's `ebook-convert` already being shelled out to on the Mac mini
  for mobi conversion. A new ingestion step per page, results cached in
  a new `panels` table:
  `book_id, page_index, panel_index, x, y, width, height` (fractions of
  the page image), reading order implied by `panel_index`.
- **No separate cropped image files.** The client's guided-view mode
  loads the same full page image already used by plain paged reading and
  applies a CSS transform (scale + translate) to frame one panel at a
  time, animating between panels on tap/arrow — this is how ComiXology
  does it under the hood, and it avoids multiplying per-page storage.
- **Confidence fallback is mandatory, not optional.** A page with no
  detected gutters (or panel boxes that don't sanely tile the page) must
  fall back to plain whole-page view rather than presenting a bad crop
  as if it were a real panel — a wrong crop is a worse experience than
  the plain page the user already has today. Store a per-page
  detection-confidence flag (or simply: null `panels` rows for that page
  = "not detected, don't offer guided mode here") rather than always
  forcing a guess.
- Reuses the reading-direction toggle from the core reader (panel order
  within a page also needs to respect left-to-right vs right-to-left).

**New dependency implication:** Kumiko is Python/OpenCV — a heavier,
different-ecosystem addition than anything else in `server/` today
(Node/TypeScript throughout, plus the one existing CLI shell-out to
Calibre's `ebook-convert`). Same shell-out pattern applies (invoke it as
a subprocess per page, parse its output), but confirm it installs
cleanly on the Mac mini before committing to it over a from-scratch
lighter-weight gutter-detection script.

**Not decided:** whether this ships as an always-on mode toggle per
comic, an explicit per-page "zoom to panels" gesture, or a reader-wide
setting — revisit once the base reader UI and real detection-accuracy
results (against actual library pages) exist to design against.

## Known gaps, deliberately not v1

Raised while auditing the plan for v1-readiness (2026-09-02), and
explicitly decided rather than accidentally dropped:

- **Accessibility (VoiceOver) has no story for the comic reader, and
  that's accepted as a future-state gap, not solved here.** `Claude.md`
  states VoiceOver support as a deliberate, explicit value elsewhere in
  the app ("audiobooks are a naturally accessibility-relevant use case,
  don't leave this implicit") — comics are the one format where that's
  genuinely hard, since a page is a raw image with no text and no
  alt-text/OCR/description layer anywhere in this plan. Worth writing
  down precisely so it reads as a conscious decision revisited later
  (an OCR-derived alt-text or panel-description pass is the likely
  eventual shape of a fix), not a silent oversight.
- **Stray `.cbr`/graphic-novel files sitting outside `/Volumes/Books/
  Comics`** (the original misfiled Dresden Files issues and Expanse
  Origins files that started this whole feature) **are explicitly out
  of scope for both the app and the conversion hand-off document** —
  Jim is moving those into the Comics source's folder manually. Neither
  `Ozzbooks_Addendum_Comics` nor `Ozzbooks_Addendum_CbrToCbzConversion`
  need to do anything about locating or relocating them.

## Testing

Per `Claude.md`'s existing testing philosophy (personal project, scope
automated tests to ingestion/parsing logic, not UI): automated coverage
belongs on `comic.ts`'s `ComicInfo.xml` parsing and filename-fallback
heuristics, and on the magic-byte detection + `scan_issues` routing for
unconverted `.cbr` files (see CBR handling above) — the same category
of "fiddly, easy to silently break, hard to notice by eye" as the
existing M4B chapter-atom and MP3-folder chapter-inference tests. The
reader UI (page fitting, zoom, spread mode) is manual click-through
verification, same as playback controls today.

**The generalized `ensureBudget()`/`getTotalCachedBytes()` (see Offline
download experience above) also needs real automated coverage, not just
manual verification** — this is existing, currently-untested-by-name
logic the shipped audio download feature depends on, being reshaped to
also cover comics and epub. A regression here silently breaks working
audio downloads, which is a materially worse failure than a new
feature shipping incomplete. Cover at minimum: eviction picks the
globally oldest item regardless of format, a whole comic issue's pages
evict together (never partially), and the existing audio-only behavior
is unchanged when no comics/epub are cached yet.

## Suggested build order

1. Schema migration: `books.format` CHECK + `page_count` column,
   `position.type` union extended to `'page'`.
2. `comic.ts` ingestion (`.cbz` only) + magic-byte-aware `.cbr` detect-
   and-skip, wired into `scan.ts`'s candidate pipeline. **Also add the
   `enrichBooks.ts` format exclusion here** (see Ingestion changes
   above) — do it alongside ingestion, not as an afterthought once
   comics are already live and enrichment has had a chance to run
   against them. Point a new source at `/Volumes/Books/Comics`, run a
   scan, verify real metadata/cover extraction against whatever's
   already `.cbz` in the real 3,440-file library before building any UI
   on top of guessed behavior. No `unrar`/Mac-mini dependency to stand
   up first.
3. Page-serving route + archive-entry-list caching, **with the page-
   load-failure/retry policy built in from the start** (see Frontend
   changes above), not bolted on after the happy path ships.
4. `ComicReader.tsx` — single-page fit-height first, then spread mode
   and pinch-zoom, then the reading-direction toggle.
5. Library UI: content-type toggle, comics grid/list mode, series
   cards + Series Detail page (see Series-browsing view above), **plus
   the `series_name` search fix** (see Library & browsing UI above) —
   small, but do it in this same pass rather than a later cleanup.
6. Offline download experience (see its own section above): generalize
   `ensureBudget()`/`getTotalCachedBytes()` across formats first, with
   its own test coverage, *before* wiring up the comics download
   action on top of it — the shared budget code is the part with the
   most blast radius if it's wrong.
7. Fast-follows: PDF graphic novels, loose image-folder comics (see
   Scope above) — only once real data shows how much of the 3,440 files
   these two minority cases actually cover — and panel-guided reading
   (see its own section above), only once the base reader has real
   usage behind it.

## Open questions to settle before/while implementing (not decided here)

- Exact `sources.type` value for the new Comics source — `'local'` or
  `'synology'` depending on how `/Volumes/Books/Comics` is actually
  mounted relative to the two existing Ozzstation sources; check against
  real config rather than assuming.
- Whether `jszip` (currently a devDependency only) has any real-world
  performance/memory concern reading large `.cbz` files (some
  omnibus-style graphic novel scans can run 40-80MB) on the Mac mini
  under concurrent requests — worth a quick real-file benchmark before
